{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 5,
  "simulation_id": "sim-02",
  "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B011",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B008",
    "B031"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I have changed my preferred configuration from B008 to B011. Nd-Fe-B magnets remain an exceptional and mature materials achievement, but the unresolved question of whether their recognition is centered on a fundamental physical discovery rather than a consequential invention, together with the need to document the two parallel contributions, is material. Optical lattice clocks offer a more sharply delimited physical architecture: magic-wavelength confinement suppresses motional clock shifts, has been realized reproducibly in independent laboratories, and has become a quantitatively reliable instrument for precision metrology and fundamental tests. Katori's originating concept and Ye's decisive experimental development form a focused and complementary attribution, provided that the citation remains confined to lattice clocks rather than the entire lineage of optical timekeeping.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary remains decisive. A prize for optical lattice clocks must not absorb indispensable preceding developments in optical frequency standards or frequency-comb technology, and the record must confirm that the proposed pair captures the central conceptual and experimental contributions. Its broadest prospective uses should also not be substituted for already demonstrated scientific merit.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll places B011 first because its magic-wavelength architecture, long-established reproducibility, and compact Katori-Ye attribution make it a mature experimental achievement.",
        "response": "I now agree with this assessment. From the standpoint of reproducible quantitative physics, the decisive feature is not simply record precision but the repeated realization of a distinct confinement principle across laboratories. That evidence makes the architecture more securely bounded at present than B008, although the historical review of its optical-clock precursors remains necessary."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson favors B007 as a mature capability for ultrafast atomic-scale observation, but identifies difficult priority and three-person attribution questions across its accelerator and facility history.",
        "response": "I agree that XFELs opened an extraordinary observational domain, with major relevance to materials and ultrafast science. However, the collective facility lineage makes it harder to connect the proposed three names to a single prize-defining advance. B011 has a clearer chain from a physical idea to reproducible realization, so I give it priority without treating support levels as a measure of scientific merit."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig argues that fluctuation theorems are deep, exact, broadly tested relations, while acknowledging uncertainty over their scope and division of credit.",
        "response": "The conceptual force of B033 is substantial, and its exact relations deserve continued consideration. I nevertheless prefer B011 because its physical principle, experimental realization, and metrological use form an unusually direct and mature discovery-to-measurement chain; B033 still requires a sharper historical delimitation among related formulations before its three-person credit can be judged."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical documentation can delimit optical lattice clocks from prior optical-frequency-standard and frequency-comb advances while confirming that Katori and Ye capture the decisive concept and experimental development.",
      "Whether the independent Nd-Fe-B developments support equal credit to Sagawa and Croat and whether that achievement is best characterized as a fundamental physical discovery.",
      "Whether the core formulations and proposed allocation of credit for fluctuation theorems can be bounded sufficiently precisely."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
